= Writing Task

*Distinguish between positive and normative economic statements, using examples related
to current unemployment rates. Assess the importance of this distinction for
economics and policymakers when analyzing labor market issues. (8 marks)*

+ *The unemployment rate is currently 4.2%. Using the figure, explain the difference
between positive and normative economic statements.* 

  A positive statement is a statement which can be proven right or wrong based on
  factually-accurate figures. For example, the unemployment rate may be 5%, and
  the latest economic report can be cited to provide a source for the statement,
  thereby proving the original figure wrong.

  A normative statement, on the other hand, is one where the statement involves a
  subjective judgement which is difficult to prove right or wrong. For example, one 
  could say that "The unemployment rate should be lower." Here, a judgement is being
  made as to whether the unemployment rate should be lower or higher. It is difficult
  to prove this statement wrong because it is subjective; i.e. it is essentially a
  matter of opinion as to whether this statement is true or false.


+ Explain what is meant by scarcity and analyze why oil is considered a scarce
  resource. (8 marks)

  - Scarcity is infinite wants but finite resources.

  - Oil is a scarce resource because it is relied upon by the entire world for energy, 
    but it is a finite resource and will eventually be used up.

  - This can be seen through the rise of oil prices and the numerous wars started over
    oil in the Middle East.
  
  - However, with the increasing need to switch to reusable energy to slow down climate
    change, oil is becoming less scarce as countries search for other, more
    carbon-neutral alternatives.


  - Opportunity cost is the cost of choosing one thing over the other; i.e. the cost is
    the next best option that you don't do.

  - The opportunity cost of choosing oil is the reduction of global warming that does
    not occur. 

  - And countries that use oil .
    
  - But the marginal cost of using oil for one more year / not phasing out oil is
    much higher than the marginal benefit. The marginal cost is the global warming
    that occurs, but the marginal benefit is the lower cost of
    the energy.
